\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{A Yukawa map and its onsite operator}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring and #{G} a finite group.
Let the \vocabk{orbit field}{fdip-00ZW} take values in
#{\mathcal O\subset F}, and let the fermion fibre #{F_\psi}
be a finite projective #{k}-module carrying a representation
#{\rho_\psi} of #{G}. A \newvocab{Yukawa map} is a #{k}-linear
map #{Y:F\to\operatorname{End}_k(F_\psi)} equivariant for
conjugation on its target. Thus
##{Y(gu)=\rho_\psi(g)Y(u)\rho_\psi(g)^{-1}.}
On the \vocabk{based cochains}{fdip-00SH} of a cellular torus,
the \newvocab{onsite Yukawa operator} #{Y_\phi} acts on the
summand at each site #{x} by the identity on the exterior
directions tensored with #{Y(\phi_x)} on the fibre. Every cell
uses its declared base corner.}

\p{Supply a perfect invariant symmetric or alternating bilinear
pairing on #{F_\psi}, or a perfect invariant Hermitian pairing
over a ring with involution. Use the resulting
\vocabk{invariant cochain pairing}{fdip-00SR}. For
#{m\in k} the fermion kernel on
#{\mathcal V=C^\bullet(X;F_\psi)} is
##{K_{U,\phi}=m\bbI+\extD_U+Y_\phi,\qquad
 \extD_U=\extd_U+\extdag_U.}
The cochain term is the self-adjoint first-order operator for the
declared pairing.}
